Q: How to implement a producer/consumer thread scheme in C using pthreads -- pthread_mutex not behaving as I expect A: <pre><code>for (...){
    pthread_mutex_lock(&amp;mxwrite);
    ...change some variables...
    pthread_cond_signal(&amp;bufready);
    pthread_mutex_unlock(&amp;mxwrite);
    /* sleep here makes it work !!?? */
    }
</code></pre>
<p>You betcha! Adding a <code>sleep()</code> call there totally changes the interleaving. Without the sleep call, almost the <em>very next thing</em> that this thread does after each time it releases the mutex is, it locks the mutex again.</p>
<p>Without diving too much into the design compromises that are faced by operating systems designers, let me just say that in the time it takes for this thread to test the loop condition and then come back around to re-lock the mutex, the other thread that's waiting  may not even have <em>started</em> to wake up. The thread running this loop most often will go around and around and around, locking and unlocking the mutex, while the other thread waits, and waits, and waits for its turn.</p>
<p>This pattern will continue until either the loop exits (thread runs out of work to be done) or it gets <em>preempted</em> by the OS scheduler. Depending on the <em>scheduling grain,</em> and the amount of work done in the loop, that could be tens of thousands of iterations of the loop before preemption.</p>
<p>A term of art that software developers use to describe this anti-pattern is <em>starvation.</em> All of the necessary conditions for the other thread to do some work have been met, but the interleaving of the program's threads <em>starve</em> it for the mutex.</p>
<p>A <code>sleep()</code> call changes everything. It forces the active thread to stop running for some definite amount of time, and during that time, the other thread gets its chance to lock the mutex.</p>
<hr />
<p><code>sleep()</code> is <em>not</em> the solution. In most cases, if your program has a problem that seems to go away when you add a <code>sleep()</code> call, the <code>sleep()</code> hasn't <em>solved</em> the problem. It only has just made the problem much less likely to rise up and bite you. In the real-world of software engineering, <code>sleep()</code> changes your problem from one that is easy to catch in testing to a problem that is very difficult to diagnose when it only happens once in a blue moon at customer sites.</p>
